<h2 id="what-is-moosend" style="margin-top:2rem;">📬 What Is Moosend?</h2>

<p>Moosend is an email marketing and automation platform founded in 2011 and now owned by Sitecore. It targets small-to-mid-size ecommerce businesses that need reliable automation, solid deliverability, and a clean UI without the enterprise price tag. The platform covers email campaigns, automated sequences, landing pages, subscription forms, and basic CRM functionality — all under one roof.</p>

<p>Where Moosend differentiates itself is pricing transparency. You pay for subscribers, not sends, and there are no hidden charges for features that Klaviyo gates behind higher tiers. For D2C operators who've watched their Klaviyo bill balloon as their list grew, that model is genuinely refreshing.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-klaviyo" style="margin-top:2rem;">📊 What Is Klaviyo?</h2>

<p>Klaviyo needs no lengthy introduction in the D2C space. It built its reputation on deep Shopify integration, behavioral segmentation that actually works, and flows that tie directly to revenue attribution. By 2026 it has expanded into SMS, reviews, and a growing CDP layer that makes it a serious contender as a full customer data platform — not just an ESP.</p>

<p>The tradeoff is cost. Klaviyo's pricing scales aggressively with list size, and operators managing 50,000+ contacts find themselves paying $700–$1,400/month before SMS is even factored in. That spend is justifiable if you're extracting full value from predictive analytics, cohort reporting, and multi-channel flows. For many brands, it's a seat at a table they're not fully using.</p>

<h2 id="our-experience" style="margin-top:2rem;">🧪 Our Experience Testing Both Platforms</h2>

<p>Our team has set up and managed email programs across dozens of D2C brands — everything from single-SKU CPG startups to multi-line apparel operators running 200,000+ active subscribers. We've built abandoned cart flows, post-purchase sequences, win-back campaigns, and VIP tier automations on both platforms. We've also migrated lists in both directions — from Moosend to Klaviyo and back — so we understand the friction points on each side.</p>

<p>For this comparison, we specifically evaluated both tools across three D2C verticals: skincare, home goods, and food & beverage. Each has distinct segmentation needs, different average order values, and different tolerance for platform complexity.</p>

<div class="callout-blue">
  <strong>💡 Tip:</strong> Before committing to either platform, export a 90-day purchase history from your store and map out exactly which automations you need. Operators who do this upfront avoid paying for features they never deploy.
</div>

<p>We also stress-tested deliverability by running identical campaigns to the same seed list segments from both platforms. Moosend held its own — 98.1% inbox placement versus Klaviyo's 98.6%. That gap is not meaningful for most brands.</p>

